# A square pyramid written with relative (negative) vertex indices, for the
# relative indices exercise. A negative index counts back from the vertices
# read so far, so the same number means different vertices on different lines.
#
# WARNING: until relative indices are supported, opening this file produces
# out-of-range vertex indices. Expect undefined behaviour in C++ and an
# exception in C#.
#
# Resolved (1-based): base 1 3 2 and 1 4 3; sides 1 2 5, 2 3 5, 3 4 5, 4 1 5.

o Pyramid

# Base corners, then the base: with four vertices read, -4 is vertex 1.
v -1 0 1
v 1 0 1
v 1 0 -1
v -1 0 -1
f -4 -2 -3
f -4 -1 -2

# The apex, then the sides: with five vertices read, -1 is the apex. The last
# two lines mix relative and absolute indices.
v 0 1.5 0
f -5 -4 -1
f -4 -3 -1
f 3 -2 -1
f -2 1 5
